    "created_at": "2026-10-17T14:35:33Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-17T14:35:33Z",
    "body": "Right, but transferring an OffscreenCanvas that doesn't have a placeholder canvas is not useful: it just serializes basic metadata (width, height, locale, text direction). So we could keep the rule simple by locking **all** OffscreenCanvases to their agent clusters, and the platform would not lose any meaningful functionality. The risk is that it could theoretically break some websites, but I doubt it (could be validated via telemetry).\n\nSide note: IMHO, holding a reference cross process is not that wild. In fact Chromium's implementation of OffscreenCanvas was meant to allow it by coordinating all communication between OffscreenCanvases and placeholder canvases using a `PlaceholderCanvasID` and a registry that maps these IDs to objects. It was meant as an IPC-friendly alternative to weak pointers. This was half implemented and abandoned due to lack of web developer interest. The main parts that are missing are the IPC services for sync'ing with out-of-process OffscreenCanvases. \n\n",
    "author_association": "MEMBER",
    "pin": null,
